1. What is Big O?

Big O notation describes how the time or space grows with input size n.

It’s about trends, not exact counts — constants and low-order terms are ignored.

🔵 In contests, time limit is usually 1 second. 🔵 Rule of thumb: 1 second = about 10⁷ to 10⁸ operations.

2. Common Time Complexities

Big O            	Description	          Typical Size of n you can handle
O(1)             	Constant time	         any n
O(log n)         	Logarithmic            up to 10¹⁸
O(√n)           	Square root time	     up to 10⁹
O(n)	            Linear	               up to 10⁷
O(n log n)      	Linearithmic	         up to 10⁵–10⁶
O(n²)	            Quadratic	             up to 10³–10⁴
O(n³)	            Cubic                	 up to 500
O(2^n)          	Exponential	           up to 20–25
O(n!)	            Factorial	             up to 10

3. When You See… Think About…

Problem Type                                                      	Good Complexity
Simple loops, arrays	                                             O(n) or O(n log n)
Sorting	                                                           O(n log n)
Dynamic Programming (easy)                                         O(n²)
Graph algorithms (BFS/DFS)	                                       O(n + m)
Shortest paths (Dijkstra)	                                         O((n + m) log n)
Bruteforce (try all subsets)	                                     O(2^n)

4. Some Must-Know Patterns
Binary Search → O(log n)

Merge Sort/Quick Sort → O(n log n)

Two pointers → O(n)

Hashmaps/Sets → O(1) average per operation

DFS/BFS → O(V + E) in graphs

DP over subsets → O(2^n * n)

5. How to Estimate If It Will Fit
When you see n:

n <= 10: you can probably do O(n!)

n <= 20: you can probably do O(2^n)

n <= 1000: you can do O(n²)

n <= 100,000: you can do O(n log n)

n <= 1,000,000: be careful, prefer O(n)

👉 Always check n before picking an approach!


📘 What is O(n)?
O(n) means "the time or space grows linearly with the size of input n."

If input size doubles, the number of operations also doubles.

🔵 It is called Big O notation — we use it to describe the performance (efficiency) of an algorithm.

✅ Simple: If n = 10, you do 10 steps; if n = 1000, you do 1000 steps.

📘 What is Time Complexity?
Time complexity measures how many operations your program needs to finish as a function of input size n.

It helps predict how fast your program will run.

✅ Example:
A loop running from 0 to n takes O(n) time, because it runs n times.

📘 What is Space Complexity?
Space complexity measures how much extra memory (RAM) your program uses based on input size n.

It considers variables, arrays, stacks, queues, recursion calls, etc.

✅ Example:
If you create an array of size n, the space complexity is O(n).

